# Repository-wide rules. The backend and frontend keep their own .gitignore
# for things specific to each.

# Deployment configuration. Holds the database password, both JWT secrets and
# the SMTP credentials - it must never reach the repository. The committed
# template beside it is .env.docker.example.
.env
.env.docker
.env.docker.*
!.env.docker.example

# Reserved Windows device names. A file called `nul` can be created by Git Bash
# translating /dev/null for a native program, and git then cannot index it -
# `git add` fails outright with "No such file or directory".
nul
con
aux
prn

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# Keys and certificates. Not used today - the deployment is plain HTTP - but
# kept so a private key dropped here by any future tooling is never committed.
# A key that reaches a repository has to be treated as compromised.
*.key
*.pem
*.crt
*.pfx

# Uploaded documents on a deployment. Real customer identity papers - they
# belong in backups, never in a repository.
data/
docs